</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effect of the number of experts.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study scaling by enabling subsets of the expert pool (1 to 7).
Fig. <a href="#S4.F6.sf3" title="In Figure 6 ‣ Effect of training loss components. ‣ 4.3 Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Mixture of Thoughts: Learning to Aggregate What Experts Think, Not Just What They Say" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(c)</span></a> shows accuracy improves steadily with more experts, despite training once on the full pool.
Top-<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> routing remains effective beyond three experts, underscoring MoT’s ability to leverage heterogeneous expertise without retraining.
Details are in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Results for number of experts ‣ Mixture of Thoughts: Learning to Aggregate What Experts Think, Not Just What They Say" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mixture of Thoughts</em> (<span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MoT</span>), a method that combines global routing with latent-level collaboration to integrate multiple heterogeneous LLMs. By enabling fine-grained hidden-state exchange through lightweight interaction layers while keeping expert backbones frozen, <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MoT</span> achieves single-pass inference that is both efficient and effective. Experiments on diverse in-distribution and out-of-distribution benchmarks show that <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MoT</span> consistently outperforms baselines, achieving state-of-the-art performance for multi-LLM systems.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
